---
// src/pages/collections/index.astro
//
// THE GUIDE TO THE COLLECTIONS. Five parts, each derived from the data so
// nothing on this page is hand-maintained:
//
//   1. Descriptive summary  extent, inclusive and bulk dates, names, surnames
//   2. Finder               names (INCLUDING variant forms) from a small
//                           inline index; photographs from Pagefind, with
//                           years matched against date INTERVALS
//   3. Holdings             one card per collection, its key photographs laid
//                           with one photograph shown whole
//   4. Chronology           a histogram: each dated photograph spread evenly
//                           across the years it could have been made in, so a
//                           date as an interval still shapes the chart. Pick
//                           a decade to see its photographs.
//   5. Surnames             a crosswalk of families against collections,
//                           which is what "arranged by custody, not by
//                           family" looks like in practice
//
// STANDING CONSTRAINTS HONOURED HERE (see the collections notes):
//   - No filter, blend, mask, backdrop or 3D. Everything that moves animates
//     transform or opacity only, and nothing moves under reduced motion.
//   - No Tailwind arbitrary values with commas or parentheses for layout.
//     Layout lives in the <style> block below, where plain CSS is safe.
//   - Nothing whose visibility rides on `hidden` gets a `display` rule.
//   - Script is wired on astro:page-load and guarded, so it survives
//     ClientRouter navigation.
//
// HOW IT LOOKS (2026-09 redesign). The page opens on the PEOPLE: a band
// of desk paper with the title, one line of lede, a wall of faces - one per
// person, their best identified face, in order of birth - and the finder.
// Each face shares its transition name with the cabinet card on that
// person's page, so it grows into it. The chronology sits on a second desk
// band; holdings and surnames on the canvas between. The surname table shows
// the twenty most photographed and folds the rest behind a button.
//
// SCALE. Rewritten 2026-09 for thousands of items. The page no longer
// carries a line, a thumbnail or an index entry per photograph: the chart is
// one path per series, and photographs are found through PAGEFIND, whose
// index is built after `astro build` and fetched in small pieces as someone
// types. The item pages declare what Pagefind reads (see [item].astro:
// data-pagefind-body, the collection / surname / year filters, the start
// sort). In `astro dev` there is no index, so photograph search and the
// decade panel say so and names still work.
//
// BUILD: package.json needs  "build": "astro build && pagefind --site dist"
// and  npm i -D pagefind.  Cloudflare runs `npm run build`, so that is all.
//
// The surname crosswalk (5) is still one row per surname; at several hundred
// surnames it will want the same treatment.
//
// 2026-09-27 PASS. Order is now: front (title, lede, finder beside THE
// TABLE; then the people) -> summary -> holdings -> surnames -> chronology
// -> colophon.
//   - THE TABLE: a handful of real prints laid on the desk the way a box of
//     family photographs comes out onto a table - true proportions, a little
//     askew, overlapping, earliest at top left. Point at one and it
//     straightens and lifts, and its label reads underneath; choose it and it
//     grows into the plate on its record (shared transition name). They are
//     dealt onto the table once on arrival; nothing moves under reduced
//     motion. Positions are seeded from the slugs, so the table is the same
//     on every build until the picks change. Sized in container units, so
//     the layout is the same at every width, only smaller.
//   - THE PEOPLE run along a rule broken at each birth decade, so the order
//     by birth is visible as generations. Fewer, larger faces.
//   - VOCABULARY: every "photograph" on the page comes from NOUN, which turns
//     to "item" the moment anything other than a photograph is described. An
//     item's kind is read from an optional `kind` field (default photograph).
//   - DECADES sit under the chart as its own axis, one column per decade,
//     when there are few enough to fit; hovering one lights its span.
//   - The surname crosswalk moved above the chronology, so the page ends on
//     the decades and their photographs, then a short colophon.
//
// 2026-10: THE MOUNT TONE. Each holdings entry rests on a pale band in its
// collection's own hue, sampled from its key print at build time, and its
// folder number takes the tone - the same tone its collection page uses for
// its frontispiece and bands. See src/data/collections/mount.ts; `mount:
// '#rrggbb'` on a collection (types.ts) pins it. Without a tone an entry looks as before.
//
// 2026-10: THE DESK. The front is now a DARK band, wider than the reading
// column (the same 76rem and the same ground as a collection's spread), and
// THE TABLE is lit by the print room: the sun over Lubbock lays a window's
// light across the desk and every print on it cuts its own shadow from the
// beam, turned as the print is turned; a lamp, and the moon, after dark. See
// src/lib/print-room.ts (drawDesk). The light is one canvas BEHIND the
// prints, drawn once on arrival, on resize and every five minutes; before
// the script runs, or without it, the band is its flat dark ground.
//   - FEWER, LARGER PRINTS: eight, in two rows, on a table that takes the
//     wider half of the band - each roughly twice the size it was.
//   - THE PEOPLE moved off the band onto the canvas below it, so the page
//     opens dark on the objects and the pale record begins with the faces.
//   - The two captions name what they show: the wall's faces are only ever
//     cut from photographs, and the table says "items" only when one of its
//     prints is not a photograph. Counts still follow NOUN.
//
// 2026-10, THE PEOPLE. The wall of faces became a ROLL OF PORTRAITS, a
// section of its own under the desk and as wide as it:
//   - Every portrait carries its NAME and the dates of its heading. Before,
//     a name appeared only under a pointer, so a phone never showed one.
//   - Under each, a LIFELINE on one scale for the whole roll: the life as a
//     thin run, the years photographed heavy over it - the holdings' date
//     bar, for a person. It is what makes "in order of birth" visible (the
//     old decade ticks are gone), and it shows how much of each life the
//     collections witness. An end with no date fades out; nothing is guessed.
//   - WHO: sixteen, not twenty-one, and every collection puts forward its
//     own most photographed before the rest of the places go by count, so an
//     early or small collection is on the roll. The note says "16 of 98".
//   - Two rows of eight on a wide screen; under 64rem ONE row that scrolls
//     sideways, so a phone swipes through the generations and no one is
//     silently dropped (it used to hide every face after the fifteenth).
//   - THAT IT SCROLLS is said on the page: the edge fades instead of
//     cutting, a line above reads "Swipe  1-3 of 16", and the row slides a
//     little and back once when it first comes into view. See .wall-swipe.
//
// 2026-10, THE FINDER reads as a field: a faint panel in a hairline, its
// glass in the band's rule colour, a ring when it has the keyboard, and a
// small "/" (which now focuses it from anywhere on the page) where there is
// a keyboard. It was a rule under italic text and was being read past.
//
// 2026-10, SECOND PASS.
//   - THE CUE. The band's last line names what is below and links to it
//     ("The holdings  278 items in 4 collections"), the same device as a
//     collection's spread: an index line, not a bouncing chevron.
//   - THE SHELF IS A STRIP OF THAT COLLECTION'S DESK. Each holdings entry
//     keeps its record on the pale band, and its prints now lie on the
//     collection's own DARK ground (--m-ground: the ground its page opens
//     on), run to the entry's edges, so the entry shows the room it leads to
//     and the key print grows into the frontispiece without the ground
//     changing under it. The strip is lit by the same sun as the table, but
//     in plain CSS: washFor() gives a gradient along the light's bearing and
//     castFor() each print's shadow. No canvas, no filter; four gradients.
//   - With the strip running the entry's full width the prints have more
//     room: the key print is taller (23rem, was 20.5rem) and the three
//     beside it larger (12rem, was 10rem).

import RootLayout from '../../layouts/RootLayout.astro';
import {
  COLLECTIONS,
  ALL_ITEMS,
  PEOPLE_WITH_ITEMS,
  itemsOf,
  keyItemsOf,
  spanOf,
  extentOf,
  personHref,
  naturalName,
  headingDates,
  FACES,
  FACE_W,
  FACE_H,
  faceHref,
} from '../../data/collections/index';
import type { Face } from '../../data/collections/index';
import { plate } from '../../data/collections/images';
import type { Rendered } from '../../data/collections/images';
import { mountTokens, mountStyle } from '../../data/collections/mount';

const EN = '\u2013';
const years = (a: number, b: number) => (a === b ? `${a}` : `${a}${EN}${b}`);
const plural = (n: number, one: string, many = `${one}s`) =>
  `${n.toLocaleString('en-US')} ${n === 1 ? one : many}`;


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
 * Vocabulary. While everything described is a photograph the page says
 * "photograph"; the first journal, letter or artifact turns every count on
 * the page to "item" and the summary breaks the total down by kind.
 * An item's kind is an OPTIONAL `kind` field on the record, read loosely
 * here so nothing breaks before types.ts has it. To add it there:
 *   kind?: 'photograph' | 'journal' | 'letter' | 'artifact';
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
const kindOf = (i: object) =>
  ((i as { kind?: string }).kind ?? 'photograph').trim().toLowerCase() || 'photograph';
const kinds = new Map<string, number>();
for (const i of ALL_ITEMS) kinds.set(kindOf(i), (kinds.get(kindOf(i)) ?? 0) + 1);
const ONLY_PHOTOS = [...kinds.keys()].every((k) => k === 'photograph');
const NOUN = ONLY_PHOTOS ? 'photograph' : 'item';
const NOUNS = `${NOUN}s`;
const Noun = NOUN[0].toUpperCase() + NOUN.slice(1);
const A_NOUN = /^[aeiou]/.test(NOUN) ? `an ${NOUN}` : `a ${NOUN}`;
const kindBreakdown = [...kinds.entries()]
  .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
  .map(([k, n]) => plural(n, k))
  .join(', ');

/* The lede. Edit freely; it is the sentence most people read. */
const LEDE =
  'Family photographs, and the papers that came down with them, described the way an archive describes its holdings: what each object is, who is in it, and how we know. Most are in my keeping; the rest I have been allowed to copy.';

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
 * Images. Rendered once per item; the item pages ask getImage() for the
 * same transforms, so this page adds no files to the build.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
// Only the photographs this page actually shows are rendered: each
// collection's key items, and one fallback for the share image. It used to
// render every item for the chronology's loupe - at thousands of items that
// was thousands of getImage() calls for pictures nobody saw here.
const keysOf = new Map(COLLECTIONS.map((c) => [c.slug, keyItemsOf(c.slug, 4)]));
const shown = new Map(
  [...[...keysOf.values()].flat(), ...(ALL_ITEMS[0] ? [ALL_ITEMS[0]] : [])].map((i) => [i.slug, i])
);
const renders = new Map<string, Rendered>(
  await Promise.all(
    [...shown.values()].map(async (i) => [i.slug, await plate(i.collection, i.recto.file, i.recto)] as const)
  )
);
const renderOf = (slug: string) => renders.get(slug) as Rendered;

// Each collection's mount tone, from its key print's smallest rung. Cached
// by slug for the whole build, so its collection page reuses the sample.
const tones = new Map(
  await Promise.all(
    COLLECTIONS.map(async (c) => {
      const k = keysOf.get(c.slug)?.[0];
      return [c.slug, await mountTokens(c.slug, c.mount, k ? renderOf(k.slug).index : undefined, Astro.site ?? Astro.url)] as const;
    })
  )
);

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
 * 1. Summary
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
const extent = extentOf(ALL_ITEMS);
const established = PEOPLE_WITH_ITEMS.filter(
  (p) => p.person.status === 'established'
).length;

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
 * 5. Surnames (computed early: the summary and the facets use it)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
const surnameRows = new Map<
  string,
  { surname: string; people: Set<string>; items: Set<string> }
>();
const surnamesOnItem = new Map<string, Set<string>>();
for (const { person, appearances } of PEOPLE_WITH_ITEMS) {
  const surname = person.surname.trim();
  const key = surname.toLowerCase();
  const row = surnameRows.get(key) ?? { surname, people: new Set(), items: new Set() };
  row.people.add(person.id);
  for (const a of appearances) {
    row.items.add(a.item.slug);
    const on = surnamesOnItem.get(a.item.slug) ?? new Set<string>();
    on.add(key);
    surnamesOnItem.set(a.item.slug, on);
  }
  surnameRows.set(key, row);
}
const itemCollection = new Map(ALL_ITEMS.map((i) => [i.slug, i.collection]));
const surnames = [...surnameRows.entries()]
  .map(([key, r]) => ({
    key,
    surname: r.surname,
    people: r.people.size,
    items: r.items.size,
    perCollection: COLLECTIONS.map(
      (c) => [...r.items].filter((s) => itemCollection.get(s) === c.slug).length
    ),
  }))
  .sort((a, b) => a.surname.localeCompare(b.surname));
const maxCell = Math.max(1, ...surnames.flatMap((r) => r.perCollection));
// The table lists the most photographed first, and shows XW_SHOW of them
// until asked for the rest; the chronology's surname menu stays alphabetical.
const XW_SHOW = 20;
const xwRows = [...surnames].sort((a, b) => b.items - a.items || a.surname.localeCompare(b.surname));

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
 * 0. The people: a roll of portraits under the desk, in order of birth
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
// One face per person, their best: identified as certain or probable, and
// the largest share of its photograph.
//
// WHO IS ON IT. Up to WALL_MAX people. Every collection first puts forward
// its own most photographed (QUOTA each, so a small or early collection is
// not crowded out by a large one); the rest of the places go to the most
// photographed overall, established headings first. A collection can only
// put forward someone who has a face marked. Ties break on the name, so the
// roll is the same on every build until the records change.
//
// THE LIFELINE under each portrait is that life on ONE scale shared by the
// whole roll (the device of the holdings' date bars): a thin run from birth
// to death, and over it, heavier, the years in which they were photographed.
// So the order by birth can be SEEN - a gap of twenty years is a step along
// the line - and so can how much of a life the collections witness. A date
// that is not established is not drawn: the run fades out at that end,
// FADE years beyond the photographs. Years come from the record's own
// `birth` / `death`, else from the dates in its heading; an "active" heading
// gives neither.
const WALL_MAX = 16;
const FADE = 12;
const FIRM: Record<string, number> = { certain: 0, probable: 1 };
const faceArea = (f: Face) => f.region.w * f.region.h;
const bestFace = new Map<string, Face>();
for (const f of FACES) {
  if (!(f.confidence in FIRM)) continue;
  const cur = bestFace.get(f.person);
  if (
    !cur ||
    FIRM[f.confidence] < FIRM[cur.confidence] ||
    (FIRM[f.confidence] === FIRM[cur.confidence] && faceArea(f) > faceArea(cur))
  ) {
    bestFace.set(f.person, f);
  }
}
const yearOf = (v?: string) => (v && /^\d{3,4}$/.test(v.trim()) ? Number(v) : undefined);
const isYear = (y: number | undefined): y is number => y !== undefined && Number.isFinite(y);
// "1834-1912", "approximately 1861-", "-1912"; "active 1885-1901" is a
// working life, not a life, and gives nothing.
const lifeFromHeading = (text: string): { born?: number; died?: number } => {
  if (/active|flourished|\bfl\b/i.test(text)) return {};
  const m = /(\d{4})?\s*[-\u2013]\s*(\d{4})?/.exec(text);
  if (!m) return {};
  return { born: m[1] ? Number(m[1]) : undefined, died: m[2] ? Number(m[2]) : undefined };
};
const candidates = PEOPLE_WITH_ITEMS.filter(({ person }) => bestFace.has(person.id)).map(
  ({ person: p, appearances }) => {
    // One entry per item, however many times the person is named on it.
    const items = [...new Map(appearances.map((a) => [a.item.slug, a.item])).values()];
    const inCollection = new Map<string, number>();
    for (const i of items) inCollection.set(i.collection, (inCollection.get(i.collection) ?? 0) + 1);
    // The years they were photographed: only the years the dates state. An
    // open date ("after 1890") has one known year, and that is the one used.
    const spans = items.map((i) => spanOf(i)).filter((x): x is NonNullable<typeof x> => !!x);
    const froms = spans.map((x) => (x.openStart ? x.to : x.from)).filter(isYear);
    const tos = spans.map((x) => (x.openEnd ? x.from : x.to)).filter(isYear);
    const heading = headingDates(p);
    const fromHeading = lifeFromHeading(heading);
    const born = yearOf(p.birth) ?? fromHeading.born;
    const died = yearOf((p as { death?: string }).death) ?? fromHeading.died;
    // Nobody is photographed outside their own life.
    let seenFrom = froms.length ? Math.min(...froms) : undefined;
    let seenTo = tos.length ? Math.max(...tos) : undefined;
    if (isYear(seenFrom) && isYear(born)) seenFrom = Math.max(seenFrom, born);
    if (isYear(seenTo) && isYear(died)) seenTo = Math.min(seenTo, died);
    if (isYear(seenFrom) && isYear(seenTo) && seenTo < seenFrom) seenTo = seenFrom;
    const dates = heading.replace(/(\d)-/g, `$1${EN}`);
    const name = naturalName(p);
    const seen = isYear(seenFrom) && isYear(seenTo) ? `, photographed ${years(seenFrom, seenTo)}` : '';
    return {
      p,
      face: bestFace.get(p.id)!,
      born,
      died,
      seenFrom,
      seenTo,
      first: seenFrom ?? Infinity,
      n: items.length,
      inCollection,
      name,
      dates,
      caption: `${name}${dates ? `, ${dates}` : ''} \u00b7 ${plural(items.length, NOUN)}${seen}`,
    };
  }
);
type OnWall = (typeof candidates)[number];
const byStanding = (a: OnWall, b: OnWall) =>
  Number(b.p.status === 'established') - Number(a.p.status === 'established') ||
  b.n - a.n ||
  a.name.localeCompare(b.name);
const QUOTA = Math.max(1, Math.floor(WALL_MAX / 2 / Math.max(1, COLLECTIONS.length)));
const chosen = new Map<string, OnWall>();
for (const c of COLLECTIONS) {
  const room = Math.min(QUOTA, WALL_MAX - chosen.size);
  if (room <= 0) break;
  const here = (w: OnWall) => w.inCollection.get(c.slug) ?? 0;
  candidates
    .filter((w) => here(w) > 0 && !chosen.has(w.p.id))
    .sort((a, b) => here(b) - here(a) || byStanding(a, b))
    .slice(0, room)
    .forEach((w) => chosen.set(w.p.id, w));
}
for (const w of [...candidates].sort(byStanding)) {
  if (chosen.size >= WALL_MAX) break;
  if (!chosen.has(w.p.id)) chosen.set(w.p.id, w);
}
// In order of birth; people with no birth year follow, in order of their
// earliest photograph.
const wall = [...chosen.values()].sort((a, b) => {
  if (a.born !== undefined && b.born !== undefined) return a.born - b.born || a.name.localeCompare(b.name);
  if (a.born !== undefined) return -1;
  if (b.born !== undefined) return 1;
  return a.first - b.first || a.name.localeCompare(b.name);
});
// The one scale: whole decades round every year on the roll.
const lifeYears = wall.flatMap((w) => [w.born, w.died, w.seenFrom, w.seenTo]).filter(isYear);
const lifeAxis = lifeYears.length
  ? {
      lo: Math.floor(Math.min(...lifeYears) / 10) * 10,
      hi: Math.ceil((Math.max(...lifeYears) + 1) / 10) * 10,
    }
  : null;
const lifePct = (y: number) => (lifeAxis ? ((y - lifeAxis.lo) / (lifeAxis.hi - lifeAxis.lo)) * 100 : 0);
const bar = (from: number, to: number) => `--x:${lifePct(from).toFixed(2)}%;--w:${(lifePct(to) - lifePct(from)).toFixed(2)}%`;
const wallCells = wall.map((w) => {
  const start = w.born ?? (isYear(w.seenFrom) && lifeAxis ? Math.max(lifeAxis.lo, w.seenFrom - FADE) : undefined);
  const end = w.died ?? (isYear(w.seenTo) && lifeAxis ? Math.min(lifeAxis.hi, w.seenTo + FADE) : undefined);
  return {
    ...w,
    run:
      isYear(start) && isYear(end) && end > start
        ? { style: bar(start, end), openStart: w.born === undefined, openEnd: w.died === undefined }
        : null,
    seen: isYear(w.seenFrom) && isYear(w.seenTo) ? { style: bar(w.seenFrom, w.seenTo + 1) } : null,
  };
});
const wallFades = wallCells.some((w) => w.run && (w.run.openStart || w.run.openEnd));
// A hairline on every lifeline at each turn of a century, so a line can be
// placed in time without a numbered axis in every cell.
const centuries = lifeAxis
  ? Array.from({ length: 30 }, (_, k) => k * 100).filter((y) => y > lifeAxis.lo && y < lifeAxis.hi)
  : [];
const lifeTicks = centuries
  .map((y) => `linear-gradient(var(--c-tick), var(--c-tick)) ${lifePct(y).toFixed(2)}% 100% / 1px 9px no-repeat`)
  .join(', ');
const crossing = surnames.filter((r) => r.perCollection.filter(Boolean).length > 1).length;

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
 * 3. Holdings. One ENTRY per collection, and the photographs stand on a
 * SHELF: the key print large, the other key items beside it, all of them at
 * their true proportions and aligned along one hairline, the way objects sit
 * on a viewing table. The mixed shapes are the point - a cabinet card, a
 * snapshot and a tintype are not the same size, and a grid of equal boxes
 * spends its time hiding that. The record reads above the shelf: title and
 * scope at the left, the figures flush right in mono.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
const cards = COLLECTIONS.map((c) => {
  const items = itemsOf(c.slug);
  const keys = keysOf.get(c.slug) ?? [];
  return {
    c,
    count: items.length,
    extent: extentOf(items),
    key: keys[0] ? { item: keys[0], r: renderOf(keys[0].slug) } : null,
    strip: keys.slice(1, 4).map((item) => ({ item, r: renderOf(item.slug) })),
  };
});

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
 * THE TABLE. A handful of prints, spread evenly across the dates, laid out
 * on the desk in a loose grid - earliest at top left - each nudged and
 * turned a little by a generator seeded from its slug, so the spread is
 * the same on every build. Every size and position is a percentage of the
 * table, and the table keeps one shape, so a phone sees the same spread
 * smaller rather than a different one.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
const TABLE_N = 8;
const TABLE_AR = 1.3; // the table's width over its height
const keySlugs = new Set([...keysOf.values()].flat().map((i) => i.slug));
const byDate = [...ALL_ITEMS].sort(
  (a, b) => (spanOf(a)?.from ?? Infinity) - (spanOf(b)?.from ?? Infinity) || a.slug.localeCompare(b.slug)
);
const tablePicks =
  byDate.length <= TABLE_N
    ? byDate
    : Array.from({ length: TABLE_N }, (_, n) => byDate[Math.round((n * (byDate.length - 1)) / (TABLE_N - 1))]);
const seeded = (text: string) => {
  let h = 2166136261;
  for (let k = 0; k < text.length; k++) h = Math.imul(h ^ text.charCodeAt(k), 16777619);
  return () => {
    h = Math.imul(h ^ (h >>> 15), h | 1);
    h ^= h + Math.imul(h ^ (h >>> 7), h | 61);
    return ((h ^ (h >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296;
  };
};
const tableCols = Math.max(2, Math.ceil(Math.sqrt(tablePicks.length * TABLE_AR)));
const tableRows = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(tablePicks.length / tableCols));
const tableRenders = await Promise.all(
  tablePicks.map((i) => plate(i.collection, i.recto.file, i.recto))
);
const table = tablePicks.map((i, n) => {
  const rnd = seeded(i.slug);
  const r = tableRenders[n];
  const ar = r.width / r.height;
  // A print's width in % of the table: mixed on purpose, and a wide print
  // is wider rather than shorter, the way a group photograph is.
  const w = Math.max(19, Math.min(39, (23 + rnd() * 7) * Math.sqrt(ar)));
  const hPct = ((w / ar) * TABLE_AR); // its height in % of the table's height
  const col = n % tableCols;
  const row = Math.floor(n / tableCols);
  // Alternate rows are staggered so the spread does not read as columns.
  const stagger = row % 2 ? 0.35 : -0.1;
  const cx = ((col + 0.5 + stagger) / tableCols) * 100 + (rnd() - 0.5) * (80 / tableCols);
  const cy = ((row + 0.5) / tableRows) * 100 + (rnd() - 0.5) * (60 / tableRows);
  const clamp = (v: number, lo: number, hi: number) => Math.max(lo, Math.min(hi, v));
  const date = (i as unknown as { date?: { display?: string } }).date?.display;
  return {
    i,
    r,
    x: clamp(cx, w / 2 + 1, 100 - w / 2 - 1),
    y: clamp(cy, hPct / 2 + 2, 100 - hPct / 2 - 2),
    w,
    rot: (rnd() - 0.5) * 14,
    z: 1 + Math.floor(rnd() * tablePicks.length),
    // The key prints already carry their transition name on the shelf
    // below; a name may only appear once on a page.
    morph: !keySlugs.has(i.slug),
    label: [i.title, date].filter(Boolean).join(', '),
  };
});
const dealOrder = table.map((_, n) => n).sort((a, b) => table[a].z - table[b].z);
// The caption names what is on the table, not what is in the collections.
const tableNoun = tablePicks.every((i) => kindOf(i) === 'photograph') ? 'photograph' : NOUN;
const TABLE_NOTE = `${plural(table.length, tableNoun)} from the boxes, earliest at top left`;

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
 * 4. Chronology
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
// How far an open-ended date ("after 1890") is spread past its one known
// year. Not a claim about when it ends - just a reach, so an open date adds a
// thin wash over the years after it rather than a spike on its one year.
const OPEN_REACH = 15;
let axis: { lo: number; hi: number } | null = null;
if (extent) {
  const lo = Math.floor(extent.from / 10) * 10;
  let hi = Math.ceil((extent.to + 1) / 10) * 10;
  if (hi - lo < 30) hi = lo + 30;
  axis = { lo, hi };
}
const pct = (y: number) => (axis ? ((y - axis.lo) / (axis.hi - axis.lo)) * 100 : 0);

// THE WEIGHTED HISTOGRAM. A photograph dated 1885-1894 adds a tenth of a
// photograph to each of those ten years; one dated 1890 adds a whole one to
// 1890. The height of a year is therefore an ESTIMATE of how many were made
// in it, and a loosely dated print cannot fake a spike. `cover` counts, per
// year, every photograph that COULD date from it - the other honest number.
const collIndex = new Map(COLLECTIONS.map((c, n) => [c.slug, n]));
const surnameIndex = new Map(surnames.map((r, n) => [r.key, n]));
const spreads = axis
  ? ALL_ITEMS.flatMap((i) => {
      const s = spanOf(i);
      if (!s || !axis) return [];
      const from = Math.max(axis.lo, s.openStart ? s.to - OPEN_REACH : s.from);
      const to = Math.min(axis.hi - 1, s.openEnd ? s.from + OPEN_REACH : s.to);
      if (to < from) return [];
      const firm = i.date.confidence === 'certain' || i.date.confidence === 'probable';
      const sn = [...(surnamesOnItem.get(i.slug) ?? [])]
        .map((k) => surnameIndex.get(k))
        .filter((n): n is number => n !== undefined);
      return [{ from, to, firm, c: collIndex.get(i.collection) ?? -1, s: sn }];
    })
  : [];
const undated = ALL_ITEMS.length - spreads.length;
const YEARS = axis ? axis.hi - axis.lo : 0;
const firmW = new Array<number>(YEARS).fill(0);
const softW = new Array<number>(YEARS).fill(0);
const cover = new Array<number>(YEARS).fill(0);
for (const sp of spreads) {
  const share = 1 / (sp.to - sp.from + 1);
  for (let y = sp.from; y <= sp.to; y++) {
    const k = y - (axis as { lo: number }).lo;
    (sp.firm ? firmW : softW)[k] += share;
    cover[k] += 1;
  }
}
const peak = Math.max(0.0001, ...firmW.map((f, k) => f + softW[k]));
// One path per series in a 0..YEARS x 0..100 box, drawn with
// preserveAspectRatio="none" so it fills whatever width the page gives it.
const H = 100;
const BAR = YEARS > 160 ? 1 : 0.86;
const r2 = (v: number) => Math.round(v * 100) / 100;
const histPath = (base: number[], top: number[]) =>
  top
    .map((v, k) => {
      if (v <= 0) return '';
      const y0 = H - (base[k] / peak) * H;
      const h = (v / peak) * H;
      return `M${k} ${r2(y0)}v${r2(-h)}h${BAR}v${r2(h)}z`;
    })
    .join('');
const zeros = new Array<number>(YEARS).fill(0);
const firmPath = histPath(zeros, firmW);
const softPath = histPath(firmW, softW);
const decades = axis
  ? Array.from({ length: YEARS / 10 }, (_, n) => {
      const from = (axis as { lo: number }).lo + n * 10;
      let est = 0;
      let could = 0;
      for (let k = n * 10; k < n * 10 + 10; k++) est += firmW[k] + softW[k];
      could = spreads.filter((sp) => sp.to >= from && sp.from <= from + 9).length;
      return { from, est: Math.round(est), could, x: pct(from), w: pct(from + 10) - pct(from) };
    })
  : [];
// Few enough decades to stand under the chart as its own axis, one column
// each? Beyond this they wrap as a row of buttons, as before.
const ALIGN_DECADES = decades.length >= 3 && decades.length <= 18;
const ticks: { year: number; x: number; label: boolean; minor: boolean }[] = [];
if (axis) {
  const step = axis.hi - axis.lo > 120 ? 20 : 10;
  for (let y = axis.lo; y <= axis.hi; y += 10) {
    const label = (y - axis.lo) % step === 0;
    ticks.push({ year: y, x: pct(y), label, minor: label && ((y - axis.lo) / step) % 2 === 1 });
  }
}
// For the client: enough to redraw the histogram for any collection or
// surname, and to read a year's figures under the pointer. Compact on
// purpose - [from, to, firm, collection, surnames] per photograph, no titles,
// no images; those come from Pagefind when someone asks for a decade.
const chronJson = JSON.stringify({
  lo: axis?.lo ?? 0,
  n: YEARS,
  peak,
  bar: BAR,
  firm: firmW.map(r2),
  soft: softW.map(r2),
  cover,
  items: spreads.map((sp) => [sp.from, sp.to, sp.firm ? 1 : 0, sp.c, sp.s]),
}).replace(/</g, '\\u003c');

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
 * 2. Finder index. Short keys because it ships in the page.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
// Names stay inline: the authority file is hundreds of records, not
// thousands, and name search has to work in dev and on the first keystroke.
// Photographs moved to Pagefind.
const finder = {
  people: PEOPLE_WITH_ITEMS.map(({ person: p, appearances }) => ({
    n: naturalName(p),
    d: headingDates(p),
    h: personHref(p.id),
    c: new Set(appearances.map((a) => a.item.slug)).size,
    // [heading, reading order, ...see-from forms]. Index 0-1 are the
    // person's own name; a hit at 2+ came through a variant.
    f: [p.authorized, naturalName(p), ...(p.variants ?? [])],
  })),
};
const finderJson = JSON.stringify(finder).replace(/</g, '\\u003c');

const busiest = [...surnames].sort((a, b) => b.items - a.items)[0];
const exampleDecade = extent
  ? Math.floor(((extent.bulk ?? extent).from + (extent.bulk ?? extent).to) / 20) * 10
  : null;
const placeholder =
  busiest && exampleDecade
    ? `Try ${busiest.surname}, or ${exampleDecade}s`
    : 'A name, a title, or a year';

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- *
 * Share image and structured data
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------- */
const shareImage =
  cards.find((c) => c.key)?.key?.r.view ??
  (ALL_ITEMS[0] ? renderOf(ALL_ITEMS[0].slug).view : undefined);

const site = Astro.site ?? Astro.url;
const abs = (path: string) => new URL(path, site).href;
const DESCRIPTION =
  'Family photographs described as archival collections: what each object is, who is in it, and how we know.';
const structured = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'Collections',
  url: abs('/collections'),
  description: DESCRIPTION,
  hasPart: cards.map(({ c, count, extent: e }) => ({
    '@type': ['Collection', 'ArchiveComponent'],
    name: c.title,
    url: abs(`/collections/${c.slug}`),
    description: c.scope,
    collectionSize: count,
    ...(e ? { temporalCoverage: `${e.from}/${e.to}` } : {}),
    ...(c.places.length
      ? { spatialCoverage: c.places.map((name) => ({ '@type': 'Place', name })) }
      : {}),
  })),
};
const structuredJson = JSON.stringify(structured).replace(/</g, '\\u003c');
---

<RootLayout
  title="Collections | Austin Allison"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  image={shareImage}
  prose={false}
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={structuredJson} />
  <script type="application/json" id="guide-finder" is:inline set:html={finderJson} />
  <script type="application/json" id="guide-chron" is:inline set:html={chronJson} />
  <noscript>
    <style is:inline>
      [data-js-only] { display: none !important; }
    </style>
  </noscript>

  <div class="guide max-w-4xl mx-auto py-6 text-iron font-serif" data-guide data-noun={NOUN}>
    <!-- 0. The front: the desk - title and finder beside the table -->
    <header class="front" data-room>
      <div class="grain" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <canvas class="room" aria-hidden="true" data-room-canvas></canvas>
      <div class="shade" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <div class="front-top">
        <div class="front-text">
          <p class="front-eyebrow">Guide</p>
          <h1 class="front-title">Collections</h1>
          <p class="lede">{LEDE}</p>
          <!-- 2. Finder -->
          <div class="finder" data-finder>
            <label for="finder-input" class="finder-label">Find a name, {A_NOUN}, or a year</label>
            <div class="finder-field">
              <svg class="finder-icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true">
                <circle cx="8.5" cy="8.5" r="5.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" />
                <path d="M12.6 12.6 17 17" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" />
              </svg>
              <input
                id="finder-input"
                type="search"
                role="combobox"
                aria-expanded="false"
                aria-controls="finder-list"
                aria-autocomplete="list"
                autocomplete="off"
                spellcheck="false"
                placeholder={placeholder}
                aria-keyshortcuts="/"
                data-finder-input
              />
              <kbd class="finder-key" aria-hidden="true">/</kbd>
            </div>
            <ul id="finder-list" class="finder-list" role="listbox" aria-label="Matches" hidden data-finder-list></ul>
            <p class="sr-only" aria-live="polite" data-finder-status></p>
          </div>
        </div>

        {table.length > 0 && (
          <figure class="table-fig" style={`--table-ar:${TABLE_AR}`}>
            <div class="spread" data-table>
              {table.map((t, n) => (
                <a
                  href={t.i.href}
                  class="spread-print"
                  style={`--x:${t.x.toFixed(2)}%;--y:${t.y.toFixed(2)}%;--w:${t.w.toFixed(2)};--r:${t.rot.toFixed(2)}deg;--z:${t.z};--i:${dealOrder.indexOf(n)}`}
                  aria-label={t.label}
                  data-label={t.label}
                >
                  {t.morph ? (
                    <img
                      src={t.r.index}
                      srcset={t.r.indexSrcset}
                      width={t.r.width}
                      height={t.r.height}
                      alt=""
                      decoding="async"
                      transition:name={`plate-${t.i.slug}`}
                    />
                  ) : (
                    <img
                      src={t.r.index}
                      srcset={t.r.indexSrcset}
                      width={t.r.width}
                      height={t.r.height}
                      alt=""
                      decoding="async"
                    />
                  )}
                </a>
              ))}
            </div>
            <figcaption class="table-cap">
              <span class="table-read" aria-hidden="true" data-table-read>{TABLE_NOTE}</span>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
        )}
      </div>

      {COLLECTIONS.length > 0 && (
        <a href="#holdings" class="cue">
          <span class="cue-text">
            <span class="cue-k">The holdings</span>
            <span class="cue-fig">
              {plural(ALL_ITEMS.length, NOUN)} in {plural(COLLECTIONS.length, 'collection')}
            </span>
          </span>
          <span class="cue-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&darr;</span>
        </a>
      )}
    </header>

    <!-- The people: a roll of portraits, in order of birth, under the desk -->
    {wallCells.length > 0 && (
      <section aria-labelledby="people-h" class="people wide" data-people>
        <div class="opener">
          <h2 id="people-h" class="opener-h">The people</h2>
          <p class="opener-fig">
            {wall.length} of {PEOPLE_WITH_ITEMS.length.toLocaleString('en-US')}
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          {wall.length < PEOPLE_WITH_ITEMS.length
            ? `${wall.length} of the ${PEOPLE_WITH_ITEMS.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} people named in the collections`
            : 'The people named in the collections'}, in order of birth.{' '}
          {lifeAxis && (
            <>
              The line under each is a life, all on one scale from {lifeAxis.lo} to {lifeAxis.hi}; the heavier part
              is the years in which they were photographed{wallFades
                ? ', and a line that fades out has no date established at that end'
                : ''}.
            </>
          )}
        </p>
        {/* Shown only where the roll is one row that scrolls. A hint for the
            eye, so it is hidden from a screen reader, which meets a list. */}
        <p class="wall-swipe" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="swipe-word">Swipe</span>
          <span class="swipe-count" data-people-count>{wallCells.length} people</span>
          <span class="swipe-arrow">&rarr;</span>
        </p>
        <div class="wall-wrap">
          <ol
            class="wall"
            aria-label="People in the photographs, in order of birth."
            style={lifeTicks ? `--life-ticks:${lifeTicks}` : undefined}
            data-people-row
          >
            {wallCells.map((w) => (
              <li class="wall-cell">
                <a href={personHref(w.p.id)} class="wall-card" aria-label={w.caption}>
                  <span class="wall-face">
                    <img
                      src={faceHref(w.face.id)}
                      srcset={`${faceHref(w.face.id)} ${FACE_W}w, ${faceHref(w.face.id, 2)} ${FACE_W * 2}w`}
                      sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 8.5rem, 7.5rem"
                      width={FACE_W}
                      height={FACE_H}
                      alt=""
                      decoding="async"
                      transition:name={`person-${w.p.id}`}
                    />
                  </span>
                  <span class="wall-name">{w.name}</span>
                  {w.dates && <span class="wall-years">{w.dates}</span>}
                  <span class="life" aria-hidden="true">
                    {w.run && (
                      <span
                        class:list={['life-run', { 'is-open-start': w.run.openStart, 'is-open-end': w.run.openEnd }]}
                        style={w.run.style}
                      />
                    )}
                    {w.seen && <span class="life-seen" style={w.seen.style} />}
                  </span>
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
        <p class="wall-foot">
          {lifeAxis && (
            <span class="wall-key" aria-hidden="true">
              <span><i class="key-run" />Lived</span>
              <span><i class="key-seen" />Photographed</span>
              <span>
                Scale {lifeAxis.lo}{EN}{lifeAxis.hi}
                {centuries.length > 0 && `, marked at ${centuries.join(' and ')}`}
              </span>
            </span>
          )}
          <a href="/collections/names" class="wall-all">
            All {PEOPLE_WITH_ITEMS.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} names <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </section>
    )}

    <!-- 1. Descriptive summary -->
    <dl class="ledger wide">
      <div>
        <dt>{ONLY_PHOTOS ? 'Photographs' : 'Items'}</dt>
        <dd class="fig">{ALL_ITEMS.length.toLocaleString('en-US')}</dd>
        <dd class="sub">
          {ONLY_PHOTOS ? '' : `${kindBreakdown}; `}in {plural(COLLECTIONS.length, 'collection')}
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Dates</dt>
        <dd class="fig">{extent ? years(extent.from, extent.to) : 'Undated'}</dd>
        <dd class="sub">
          {extent?.bulk
            ? `bulk ${years(extent.bulk.from, extent.bulk.to)}`
            : extent && extent.undated > 0
              ? `${extent.undated} undated`
              : 'inclusive'}
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>People named</dt>
        <dd class="fig">{PEOPLE_WITH_ITEMS.length.toLocaleString('en-US')}</dd>
        <dd class="sub">{established} with established headings</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Surnames</dt>
        <dd class="fig">{surnames.length.toLocaleString('en-US')}</dd>
        <dd class="sub">
          {COLLECTIONS.length > 1 ? `${crossing} in more than one collection` : 'in the collection'}
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <!-- 3. Holdings -->
    <section id="holdings" aria-labelledby="holdings-h" class="sect wide cue-target">
      <div class="opener">
        <h2 id="holdings-h" class="opener-h">Holdings</h2>
        <p class="opener-fig">{plural(COLLECTIONS.length, 'collection')}</p>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Each collection is a group of {NOUNS} that came to me together,
        so a collection is not the same thing as a family: one box can hold
        several families, and one family can turn up in several boxes. The
        people in them are gathered across every collection in the <a
          href="/collections/names"
          class="text-caprock hover:underline">name authority file</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="entries">
        {cards.map(({ c, count, extent: e, key, strip }, n) => (
          <article class="entry" style={mountStyle(tones.get(c.slug) ?? null)} data-collection={c.slug}>
            <div class="entry-mark" aria-hidden="true">{String(n + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</div>
            <div class="entry-head">
              <div class="entry-text">
                <h3 class="entry-title">
                  <a href={`/collections/${c.slug}`} class="entry-link">{c.title}</a>
                </h3>
                <p class="entry-scope">{c.scope}</p>
              </div>

              <div class="entry-figures">
                <p class="fig-line"><b>{count.toLocaleString('en-US')}</b> {count === 1 ? NOUN : NOUNS}</p>
                {e && <p class="fig-line"><b>{years(e.from, e.to)}</b></p>}
                {e?.bulk && <p class="fig-line fig-quiet">bulk <b>{years(e.bulk.from, e.bulk.to)}</b></p>}
                {e && axis && (
                  /* The collection's span drawn on the same axis as the
                     chronology below, so the entries can be read against one
                     another at a glance. */
                  <span class="spark" aria-hidden="true">
                    <span class="spark-run" style={`--x:${pct(e.from).toFixed(2)}%;--w:${(pct(e.to + 1) - pct(e.from)).toFixed(2)}%`}></span>
                    {e.bulk && (
                      <span class="spark-bulk" style={`--x:${pct(e.bulk.from).toFixed(2)}%;--w:${(pct(e.bulk.to + 1) - pct(e.bulk.from)).toFixed(2)}%`}></span>
                    )}
                  </span>
                )}
                {c.places.length > 0 && <p class="fig-line fig-quiet">{c.places.join('; ')}</p>}
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="shelf" data-shelf>
              {key ? (
                <img
                  class="plate plate--key"
                  style={`--ar:${(key.r.width / key.r.height).toFixed(4)}`}
                  src={key.r.view}
                  srcset={key.r.viewSrcset}
                  sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 340px, 62vw"
                  width={key.r.width}
                  height={key.r.height}
                  alt=""
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                  transition:name={`plate-${key.item.slug}`}
                />
              ) : (
                <span class="plate-empty">Nothing described yet</span>
              )}
              {strip.map(({ item, r }) => (
                <img
                  class="plate plate--small"
                  style={`--ar:${(r.width / r.height).toFixed(4)}`}
                  src={r.index}
                  srcset={r.indexSrcset}
                  sizes="(min-width: 48rem) 150px, 26vw"
                  width={r.width}
                  height={r.height}
                  alt=""
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                  transition:name={`plate-${item.slug}`}
                />
              ))}
            </div>

            <p class="entry-foot">
              <span>{c.lines.length > 0 ? c.lines.join(', ') : ''}</span>
              <span class="entry-go">Browse the collection <span class="entry-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></span>
            </p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 5. Surnames -->
    {surnames.length > 0 && COLLECTIONS.length > 0 && (
      <section aria-labelledby="surnames-h" class="sect wide">
        <div class="opener">
          <h2 id="surnames-h" class="opener-h">Surnames across the collections</h2>
          <p class="opener-fig">{plural(surnames.length, 'surname')}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="xw-grid">
        <p class="note">
          Collections follow where the {NOUNS} came from, so families run
          across them. Figures are {NOUNS}; the surnames that turn up most
          come first.
        </p>
        <div class="xwalk-wrap">
          <table class="xwalk" data-xw>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Surname</th>
                {COLLECTIONS.map((c) => <th scope="col" class="num">{c.title}</th>)}
                <th scope="col" class="num">People</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {xwRows.map((r, i) => (
                <tr class:list={[{ 'xw-more': i >= XW_SHOW }]}>
                  <th scope="row">{r.surname}</th>
                  {r.perCollection.map((n) => (
                    <td class="num">
                      {n > 0 ? (
                        <span class="tally" style={`--v:${(n / maxCell).toFixed(3)}`}>{n}</span>
                      ) : (
                        <>
                          <span class="nil" aria-hidden="true">&ndash;</span>
                          <span class="sr-only">none</span>
                        </>
                      )}
                    </td>
                  ))}
                  <td class="num">{r.people}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        {xwRows.length > XW_SHOW && (
          <button
            type="button"
            class="xw-toggle"
            aria-expanded="false"
            data-xw-toggle
            data-more={`Show all ${xwRows.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} surnames`}
            data-less={`Show the first ${XW_SHOW}`}
            data-js-only
          >
            Show all {xwRows.length.toLocaleString('en-US')} surnames
          </button>
        )}
        </div>
      </section>
    )}
    <!-- 4. Chronology -->
    {axis && spreads.length > 0 && (
      <section aria-labelledby="chron-h" class="bleed chron-band wide" data-chron>
        <div class="opener">
          <h2 id="chron-h" class="opener-h">Chronology</h2>
          {extent && <p class="opener-fig">{years(extent.from, extent.to)}</p>}
        </div>
        <p class="note">
          How many {NOUNS} were made in each year, as far as the dates
          allow. One dated only to a span of years is spread evenly across
          it, so a loosely dated print adds a little to many years rather
          than all of itself to one. Choose a decade to see what it holds.
        </p>

        <div class="facets" data-js-only>
          {COLLECTIONS.length > 1 && (
            <div class="facet-row" role="group" aria-label={`Show ${NOUNS} from a collection`}>
              <span class="facet-label" aria-hidden="true">Collection</span>
              <button type="button" class="facet" aria-pressed="true" data-facet="c" data-value="" data-label="All collections">All</button>
              {COLLECTIONS.map((c, n) => (
                <button type="button" class="facet" aria-pressed="false" data-facet="c" data-value={String(n)} data-slug={c.slug} data-label={c.title}>
                  {c.title}
                </button>
              ))}
            </div>
          )}
          {surnames.length > 0 && (
            <div class="facet-row">
              {/* A native select, not a row of buttons: it holds three
                  surnames or three hundred in the same space, and it is
                  already keyboard- and screen-reader-complete. */}
              <label for="chron-surname" class="facet-label">Surname</label>
              <select id="chron-surname" class="facet-select" data-surname>
                <option value="">All surnames</option>
                {surnames.map((r, n) => (
                  <option value={String(n)} data-key={r.key}>{r.surname} ({r.items})</option>
                ))}
              </select>
            </div>
          )}
        </div>

        <div class:list={['hist', { 'is-aligned': ALIGN_DECADES }]} data-hist>
          <div class="hist-plot">
            <div class="rules" aria-hidden="true">
              {ticks.map((t) => (
                <span class:list={['tick', { minor: t.minor }]} style={`--x:${t.x.toFixed(3)}%`}>
                  {t.label && <b>{t.year}</b>}
                </span>
              ))}
            </div>
            <svg
              class="hist-svg"
              viewBox={`0 0 ${YEARS} ${H}`}
              preserveAspectRatio="none"
              role="img"
              aria-label={`${Noun}s by year, ${axis.lo} to ${axis.hi - 1}. The decade list below gives the figures.`}
              data-hist-svg
            >
              <path class="h-firm" d={firmPath} data-h-firm />
              <path class="h-soft" d={softPath} data-h-soft />
              <path class="h-lit" d="" data-h-lit />
            </svg>
            <span class="hist-sel" aria-hidden="true" hidden data-hist-sel></span>
            <span class="hist-cursor" aria-hidden="true" hidden data-hist-cursor></span>
          </div>

          {/* The decades are the way in, for every kind of reader: real
              buttons with real figures, so the chart itself can stay a
              picture. When they fit, they ARE the chart's axis: one column
              under each decade of bars. */}
          <div
            class:list={['decades', { 'decades--axis': ALIGN_DECADES }]}
            style={`--n:${decades.length}`}
            role="group"
            aria-label="Browse by decade"
          >
            {decades.map((d) => (
              <button
                type="button"
                class="decade"
                aria-pressed="false"
                aria-controls="decade-panel"
                data-decade={d.from}
                disabled={d.could === 0}
              >
                <span class="decade-y">{d.from}s</span>
                <span class="decade-n" data-decade-n>{d.could > 0 ? `~${d.est}` : '\u2013'}</span>
              </button>
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="hist-readout" aria-hidden="true" data-hist-readout>&nbsp;</p>
        </div>

        <div class="legend">
          <span><i class="swatch firm"></i>Certain or probable date</span>
          <span><i class="swatch soft"></i>Possible date</span>
          {undated > 0 && <span>{plural(undated, NOUN)} undated, not counted</span>}
        </div>

        <div id="decade-panel" class="panel" hidden data-panel>
          <div class="panel-head">
            <h3 class="panel-title" data-panel-title></h3>
            <button type="button" class="panel-close" data-panel-close aria-label="Close the decade">&#10005;</button>
          </div>
          <ul class="panel-grid" data-panel-grid></ul>
          <p class="panel-note" data-panel-note hidden></p>
          <button type="button" class="panel-more" data-panel-more hidden>Show more</button>
        </div>

        <p class="sr-only" aria-live="polite" data-chron-status></p>
      </section>
    )}


    <!-- Colophon -->
    <div class="colophon wide">
      <p>
        Every {NOUN} here is published as IIIF, so its images open at full
        resolution in any IIIF viewer, such as Mirador or the Universal Viewer.
        Names follow the <a href="/collections/names" class="text-caprock hover:underline">name
        authority file</a>, and a date is given as a range wherever the evidence
        allows nothing closer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</RootLayout>

<style>
  .guide {
    --c-caprock: var(--color-caprock, #9c5b3f);
    --c-sage: var(--color-sage, #7a8471);
    --c-haze: var(--color-haze, #dad3c4);
    --c-dust: var(--color-dust, #ede7da);
    --c-iron: var(--color-iron, #3b3833);
    --c-canvas: var(--color-canvas, #f7f4ec);
    --mono: var(--font-mono, ui-monospace, 'IBM Plex Mono', monospace);
  }
  .guide [hidden] {
    display: none !important;
  }
  .guide {
    --c-mount: var(--color-caliche, #e8e1d1);
    --c-desk: var(--color-desk, #f5f3ed);
    /* THE PAGE'S ONE MEASURE: up to 76rem, never past the window less
       2.5rem (so no sideways scroll). */
    --fw: min(76rem, 100vw - 2.5rem);
  }
  /* Every part of the guide is set to that measure, whatever the column the
     layout gives the page: the box is given the width outright and centred
     by equal (usually negative) side margins - the device of a collection's
     spread. Running text inside keeps a reading measure of its own. */
  .wide {
    width: var(--fw);
    margin-left: calc((100% - var(--fw)) / 2);
    margin-right: calc((100% - var(--fw)) / 2);
  }
  .sect {
    margin-top: clamp(3.5rem, 6.5vw, 5.75rem);
  }
  /* SECTION OPENERS. The heading at display size - the face is at its best
     large, and the title was the page's only large type - on a hairline that
     begins with a short heavy stroke of caprock, with one fact about the
     section in mono at the far end of the line, as a ledger's running head
     carries its folio. */
  .opener {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.3rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.15rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.7rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
  }
  .opener::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    bottom: -1px;
    width: 2.75rem;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--c-caprock);
  }
  .opener-h {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.3rem + 2.6vw, 3.3rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.02;
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .opener-fig {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--c-iron);
    font: 0.72rem/1.4 var(--mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    white-space: nowrap;
    opacity: 0.74;
  }
  /* FULL-WIDTH BANDS without a 100vw element: painted by border-image
     outset, which is ink overflow - it reaches the window edges and never
     adds a horizontal scrollbar. The same device as the person pages. */
  .bleed {
    border-image: conic-gradient(var(--c-desk) 0 0) fill 0 / / 0 100vmax;
  }
  /* 0. The front: THE DESK ------------------------------------------ */
  /* The dark ground and its inks. These are the values a collection's
     spread falls back to when it has no mount tone of its own, so the guide
     and the pages it leads to are the same room. */
  .guide {
    --c-ground: #26221d;
    --c-ink: #efe8da;
    --c-quiet: #b3ab9c;
    --c-rule: #b8957c;
    /* The grain: light and dark specks at four strengths on a clear
       ground, tiled. Used by the band (.grain) and by each holdings strip. */
    --grain: url('data:image/png;base64,');
  }
  /* Wider than the reading column (up to 76rem, never past the window less
     2.5rem, so no sideways scroll) and carried to the window edges by the
     same border-image outset as the bands. The negative top margin cancels
     the page's py-6, so the ground meets the header. No filter, blend, mask
     or 3D: the light is a canvas, the shade a gradient. */
  .front {
    position: relative;
    width: var(--fw);
    margin: -1.5rem calc((100% - var(--fw)) / 2) 0;
    padding: clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 3.5rem) 0 0;
    border-image: conic-gradient(var(--c-ground) 0 0) fill 0 / / 0 100vmax;
    color: var(--c-ink);
  }
  /* THE DESK'S SURFACE. A fine grain under the light, so the beam falls on
     a material and not on a flat colour. It is one small tiled image (96px,
     4.6kB, in --grain below) - NOT a filter - and --grain-strength is its
     dial (0 is the flat ground again). The band's ground runs on to the
     window edges as flat colour, so here the grain is let go before the
     box's own edges: a wash of the ground colour is laid over it there. */
  .grain {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: var(--grain-strength, 1);
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(to right, var(--c-ground), transparent 9%, transparent 91%, var(--c-ground)), var(--grain);
  }
  /* The print room's light: a transparent canvas filling the band's box,
     under everything in it. It fades in once drawn, so a slow script never
     shows a half-lit room. */
  .room {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: 0;
    /* Held back until the prints have been dealt: the light comes up on a
       table that is already laid, not on shadows waiting for their prints. */
    transition: opacity 0.9s ease 0.6s;
  }
  .room.is-lit {
    opacity: 1;
  }
  /* THE SHADE. The window is over the table; the title sits back from it.
     A wash of the ground's own colour, laid over the light and under the
     words, so the beam fades out toward the text rather than crossing it at
     full strength - the small mono labels stay readable at noon. SHADE is
     the dial: 0 lets the light fall everywhere, 1 keeps it off the words. */
  .shade {
    --shade: 0.78;
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    opacity: var(--shade);
    background: linear-gradient(to bottom, var(--c-ground) 0, var(--c-ground) 30%, transparent 62%);
  }
  .front > :not(.room):not(.shade):not(.grain) {
    position: relative;
  }
  .front-eyebrow {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--c-rule);
    font: 0.72rem/1.4 var(--mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .front-title {
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(2.9rem, 1.2rem + 4.2vw, 5.25rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 0.95;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .front .lede {
    max-width: 31rem;
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    opacity: 0.88;
  }

  /* THE FRONT, in two columns on a wide screen: the words and the finder
     at left, the table at right with the wider share. */
  .front-top {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2.25rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 52rem) {
    .front-top {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.82fr) minmax(0, 1.18fr);
      align-items: center;
      gap: clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 4.5rem);
    }
    .shade {
      background: linear-gradient(to right, var(--c-ground) 0, var(--c-ground) 22%, transparent 52%);
    }
  }
  /* THE FINDER, on the dark ground. It used to be a rule under a line of
     italic - correct, and easy to read past. Now it is A FIELD: a faint
     lighter panel inside a hairline, so it has the outline of something to
     type in, with its glass in the band's warm rule colour. It answers the
     pointer (the hairline firms) and takes a ring of the rule colour when
     it has the keyboard. On a machine with a keyboard a small "/" at its
     right says the field can be reached by that key. Nothing moves and
     nothing is brighter than the title.
     Its list of matches keeps the page's own paper and ink (it must name
     its colour: it would inherit the band's). */
  .front .finder {
    margin-top: 1.9rem;
  }
  .front .finder-label {
    color: var(--c-ink);
    opacity: 0.8;
  }
  .front .finder-field {
    margin-top: 0.55rem;
    border: 1px solid rgb(239 232 218 / 0.3);
    background: rgb(239 232 218 / 0.07);
    transition:
      border-color 0.2s ease,
      background-color 0.2s ease,
      box-shadow 0.2s ease;
  }
  .front .finder-field:hover {
    border-color: rgb(239 232 218 / 0.5);
  }
  .front .finder-field:focus-within {
    border-color: var(--c-rule);
    background: rgb(239 232 218 / 0.1);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--c-rule);
  }
  .front .finder-icon {
    left: 0.85rem;
    width: 1.2rem;
    height: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--c-rule);
  }
  .front .finder input {
    padding: 0.7rem 2.9rem 0.65rem 2.65rem;
    border-bottom: 0;
  }
  .front .finder input::placeholder {
    color: var(--c-ink);
    opacity: 0.6;
  }
  .front .finder input:focus {
    border-bottom: 0;
    box-shadow: none;
  }
  .front .finder-list {
    z-index: 40;
    color: var(--c-iron);
  }

  /* THE TABLE. It keeps one shape; every print is placed and sized in
     percentages of it, centred on its point and turned a few degrees.
     Pointing at one straightens it, lifts it to the top of the pile and
     reads its label underneath. It is as wide as its column, but never so
     tall that the band outgrows the window. */
  .table-fig {
    width: min(100%, calc(66vh * var(--table-ar, 1.3)));
    width: min(100%, calc(66svh * var(--table-ar, 1.3)));
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  .spread {
    position: relative;
    aspect-ratio: var(--table-ar, 1.3);
  }
  /* Each print lies on a narrow off-white mat, so a scan that shows a
     sliver of the scanner's bed reads as a print with a border rather than
     a print with a white edge on one side. Its shadow is the print room's
     (--cast, lying; --cast-up, lifted), set by the script for the light at
     the visit; the values written here are the room before it is lit. */
  .spread-print {
    position: absolute;
    left: var(--x);
    top: var(--y);
    z-index: var(--z);
    box-sizing: border-box;
    width: calc(var(--w) * 1%);
    padding: clamp(2px, 0.4vw, 5px);
    background: var(--c-mount);
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(var(--r));
    box-shadow: var(--cast, 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.5), 0 16px 26px -12px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.75));
    transition:
      transform 0.3s ease,
      box-shadow 0.3s ease;
    animation: deal 0.7s cubic-bezier(0.2, 0.7, 0.2, 1) backwards;
    animation-delay: calc(var(--i) * 75ms + 120ms);
  }
  .spread-print img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .spread-print:hover,
  .spread-print:focus-visible {
    z-index: 30;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(0deg) scale(1.08);
    box-shadow: var(--cast-up, 0 2px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.5), 0 30px 44px -16px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.85));
  }
  .spread-print:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--c-rule);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }
  /* Dealt onto the table: each print arrives from a little above, turned
     further, and settles. Transform and opacity only. */
  @keyframes deal {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translate(-50%, -50%) translateY(-2.5rem) rotate(calc(var(--r) * 2.5)) scale(1.05);
    }
  }
  .table-cap {
    min-height: 1.5em;
    margin-top: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--c-quiet);
    font: 0.7rem/1.5 var(--mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  }
  .table-read.is-reading {
    color: var(--c-ink);
  }

  /* THE CUE. The band's last line: what is below, how much, and a link to
     it - as on a collection's spread. Its arrow nudges twice and is then
     still; not at all under reduced motion. */
  .cue {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 1.9rem);
    padding: 0.85rem 0 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgb(255 255 255 / 0.14);
    color: var(--c-quiet);
    font: 0.72rem/1.4 var(--mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-decoration: none;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  /* The words may wrap among themselves on a phone; the arrow keeps its
     place at the right of the first line. */
  .cue-text {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.25rem 1rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .cue-k {
    color: var(--c-rule);
  }
  .cue-arrow {
    display: inline-block;
    flex: none;
    margin-left: auto;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--c-ink);
    animation: cue-nudge 1.4s ease-in-out 1.6s 2;
  }
  .cue:hover .cue-k,
  .cue:hover .cue-fig,
  .cue:hover .cue-arrow {
    color: var(--c-ink);
  }
  .cue:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--c-rule);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }
  @keyframes cue-nudge {
    0%,
    100% {
      transform: none;
    }
    45% {
      transform: translateY(0.3rem);
    }
  }
  /* Where the cue lands: clear of the sticky header. */
  .cue-target {
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--header-h, 5.5rem) + 1rem);
  }

  /* THE PEOPLE. A roll of portraits in order of birth, as wide as the desk
     above it (the same 76rem, without the ground), so its left edge is the
     title's. Each portrait is a mounted crop with the name and the heading's
     dates beneath it and, at the foot of the cell, that life on the roll's
     one scale. No name is hidden behind a pointer: a phone reads it too.
       WIDE: eight to a row, so sixteen people are two rows.
       NARROWER (under 64rem): ONE row that scrolls sideways - on a phone, a
     swipe through the generations rather than five rows to scroll past -
     with the next portrait showing at the edge to say there is more. */
  .people {
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 5.5vw, 4.5rem);
  }
  /* THAT THE ROW SCROLLS is said four ways, none of them a bouncing chevron:
     the next portrait is cut by the edge; the edge is a FADE, so the cut
     reads as "goes on" rather than "ends"; a line above the row says "Swipe"
     and counts where you are ("1-3 of 16"); and once, when the row first
     comes into view, it slides a little to the left and settles back. After
     the first swipe the word goes and the count stays. All of it is absent
     where the roll is a grid, and where the row happens not to overflow. */
  .wall-swipe {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0;
    color: var(--c-iron);
    font: 0.7rem/1.5 var(--mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .swipe-word,
  .swipe-arrow {
    color: var(--c-caprock);
  }
  .swipe-count {
    opacity: 0.74;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .people[data-swiped] .swipe-word,
  .people[data-at='end'] .swipe-arrow {
    display: none;
  }
  .people[data-at='none'] .wall-swipe {
    display: none;
  }
  .wall-wrap {
    position: relative;
  }
  /* The fades: the page's own paper laid over the row's two edges. The
     right one is there until the last portrait is reached; the left one
     appears once the first has gone. Without script only the right shows. */
  .wall-wrap::before,
  .wall-wrap::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    bottom: 0.6rem;
    z-index: 1;
    width: 2.75rem;
    pointer-events: none;
    transition: opacity 0.2s ease;
  }
  .wall-wrap::before {
    left: -4px;
    background: linear-gradient(to left, transparent, var(--c-canvas));
    opacity: 0;
  }
  .wall-wrap::after {
    right: -4px;
    background: linear-gradient(to right, transparent, var(--c-canvas));
  }
  .people[data-at='mid'] .wall-wrap::before,
  .people[data-at='end'] .wall-wrap::before {
    opacity: 1;
  }
  .people[data-at='end'] .wall-wrap::after,
  .people[data-at='none'] .wall-wrap::after {
    opacity: 0;
  }
  /* The one slide, on first sight. Every cell moves alike, so it is the
     row that is seen to move; transform only. */
  .people.is-seen:not([data-at='none']) .wall-cell {
    animation: row-peek 1.1s cubic-bezier(0.3, 0, 0.2, 1) 0.35s 1;
  }
  @keyframes row-peek {
    0%,
    100% {
      transform: none;
    }
    42% {
      transform: translateX(-2rem);
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .wall-swipe,
    .wall-wrap::before,
    .wall-wrap::after {
      display: none;
    }
    .people.is-seen .wall-cell {
      animation: none;
    }
  }
  .wall {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0 1rem;
    margin: 0.45rem -4px 0;
    padding: 0.3rem 4px 0.9rem;
    list-style: none;
    overflow-x: auto;
    scroll-snap-type: x proximity;
    scroll-padding-inline: 4px;
    scrollbar-width: thin;
    scrollbar-color: var(--c-haze) transparent;
    /* The century marks' ink. Declared HERE, beside --life-ticks (set on
       this element by the page): a custom property's var() is resolved
       where the property is declared, not where it is used. */
    --c-tick: rgb(59 56 51 / 0.45);
  }
  .wall-cell {
    display: flex;
    flex: none;
    width: 7.5rem;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
  }
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .wall {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(8, minmax(0, 1fr));
      gap: 2.1rem 1rem;
      margin-top: 1.4rem;
      padding-bottom: 0.3rem;
      overflow: visible;
    }
    .wall-cell {
      width: auto;
    }
  }
  @media (min-width: 76rem) {
    .wall {
      column-gap: 1.35rem;
    }
  }
  /* The whole cell is the link. It is a column, and the lifeline is pushed
     to its foot, so the lines of a row sit level however many lines the
     names above them take - they are one chart, read across. */
  .wall-card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    width: 100%;
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: opacity 0.25s ease;
  }
  .wall-face {
    display: block;
    padding: 3px;
    background: var(--c-mount);
    border: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
    box-shadow: 0 8px 14px -12px rgb(26 25 25 / 0.6);
    transition:
      transform 0.25s ease,
      border-color 0.25s ease;
  }
  .wall-face img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .wall-name {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.65rem;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: 1.18;
    text-wrap: balance;
    transition: color 0.25s ease;
  }
  /* Iron, not sage: this is small type that carries a fact. */
  .wall-years {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
    color: var(--c-iron);
    font: 0.66rem/1.4 var(--mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    opacity: 0.74;
  }
  .wall-card:hover .wall-face,
  .wall-card:focus-visible .wall-face {
    border-color: var(--c-caprock);
    transform: translateY(-3px);
  }
  .wall-card:hover .wall-name,
  .wall-card:focus-visible .wall-name {
    color: var(--c-caprock);
  }
  .wall-card:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--c-caprock);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }
  .wall:has(.wall-card:hover) .wall-card:not(:hover) {
    opacity: 0.55;
  }
  /* THE LIFELINE: the holdings' date bar, for a person. A thin run is the
     life; the heavy part, the years photographed. An end with no date fades
     out instead of stopping. */
  /* The room above the line is the line's own padding (its bars hang from
     the bottom edge), so it survives `margin-top: auto` being nothing in
     the cell with the longest name. */
  .life {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    box-sizing: content-box;
    height: 7px;
    margin-top: auto;
    padding-top: 0.7rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
    background: var(--life-ticks, none);
  }
  .life-run,
  .life-seen {
    position: absolute;
    bottom: 0;
    left: var(--x);
    width: var(--w);
    min-width: 3px;
  }
  .life-run {
    bottom: 2px;
    height: 3px;
    background: rgb(156 91 63 / 0.5);
  }
  .life-run.is-open-end {
    background: linear-gradient(to right, rgb(156 91 63 / 0.5) 55%, rgb(156 91 63 / 0));
  }
  .life-run.is-open-start {
    background: linear-gradient(to left, rgb(156 91 63 / 0.5) 55%, rgb(156 91 63 / 0));
  }
  .life-run.is-open-start.is-open-end {
    background: linear-gradient(to right, rgb(156 91 63 / 0), rgb(156 91 63 / 0.5) 30% 70%, rgb(156 91 63 / 0));
  }
  .life-seen {
    height: 7px;
    background: var(--c-caprock);
  }
  .wall-foot {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.4rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
    color: var(--c-iron);
    font: 0.7rem/1.5 var(--mono);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .wall-key {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.3rem 1.25rem;
    opacity: 0.74;
  }
  .wall-key i {
    display: inline-block;
    width: 1.5rem;
    margin-right: 0.5rem;
    vertical-align: middle;
  }
  .key-run {
    height: 3px;
    background: rgb(156 91 63 / 0.5);
  }
  .key-seen {
    height: 7px;
    background: var(--c-caprock);
  }
  .wall-all {
    margin-left: auto;
    color: var(--c-caprock);
    text-decoration: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .wall-all:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* The colophon that closes the page. */
  .colophon {
    margin-top: clamp(3rem, 5vw, 4.5rem);
    padding-top: 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
  }
  .colophon p {
    max-width: 40rem;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.55;
    opacity: 0.78;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* The chronology's band. */
  .chron-band {
    margin-top: clamp(3.5rem, 6.5vw, 5.75rem);
    padding: clamp(2.5rem, 4.5vw, 3.75rem) 0 clamp(2.75rem, 4.5vw, 3.75rem);
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .wall-card,
    .wall-face,
    .wall-name,
    .spread-print {
      transition: none;
      animation: none;
    }
    .front .finder-field,
    .finder-key {
      transition: none;
    }
    .people.is-seen .wall-cell {
      animation: none;
    }
    .room {
      transition: none;
    }
    .cue-arrow {
      animation: none;
    }
    .spread-print:hover,
    .spread-print:focus-visible {
      transform: translate(-50%, -50%) rotate(0deg);
    }
    .wall-card:hover .wall-face,
    .wall-card:focus-visible .wall-face {
      transform: none;
    }
  }
  @media print {
    .bleed {
      border-image: none;
    }
    /* The desk prints as paper: no ground, no light, the page's own ink. */
    .front {
      width: auto;
      margin: 0 0 1rem;
      padding-top: 0;
      border-image: none;
      color: var(--c-iron);
    }
    .room,
    .shade,
    .grain {
      display: none;
    }
    .front .front-eyebrow,
    .front .finder-label,
    .table-cap {
      color: var(--c-sage);
    }
    .spread-print,
    .plate {
      box-shadow: none;
      border: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
    }
    .cue {
      display: none;
    }
    .wall-swipe,
    .wall-wrap::before,
    .wall-wrap::after {
      display: none;
    }
    /* The roll prints whole, wrapped, at the page's own width. */
    .people {
      width: auto;
      margin-inline: 0;
    }
    .wall {
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      row-gap: 1.25rem;
      overflow: visible;
    }
    .shelf {
      margin-inline: 0;
      padding-inline: 0;
      background: none;
      color: inherit;
    }
    .wide {
      width: auto;
      margin-left: 0;
      margin-right: 0;
    }
  }

  .lede,
  .note {
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .note {
    max-width: 40rem;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  /* 1. Summary ---------------------------------------------------- */
  /* Four figures at display size: the whole of the holdings in one line of
     the page, ruled like a ledger's totals. */
  .ledger {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 4.5vw, 3.75rem);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
  }
  .ledger > div {
    padding: 1rem 0.75rem 1.1rem 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
  }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .ledger {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
    .ledger > div {
      padding: 1.25rem 1rem 1.4rem 0;
    }
    .ledger > div + div {
      padding-left: 1.5rem;
      border-left: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
    }
  }
  .ledger dt {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-iron);
    opacity: 0.7;
  }
  .ledger .fig {
    margin: 0.45rem 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 1.15rem + 3.3vw, 4.1rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 0.95;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    font-variant-numeric: oldstyle-nums proportional-nums;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .ledger .sub {
    margin: 0.55rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-style: italic;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  /* 2. Finder ----------------------------------------------------- */
  .finder {
    position: relative;
    max-width: 34rem;
    margin-top: 2rem;
  }
  .finder-label {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-sage);
  }
  .finder-field {
    position: relative;
  }
  .finder-icon {
    position: absolute;
    left: 0.1rem;
    top: 50%;
    width: 1.1rem;
    height: 1.1rem;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    color: var(--c-sage);
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .finder input {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.25rem 0.45rem 1.75rem;
    border: 0;
    border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--c-iron);
    border-radius: 0;
    background: transparent;
    color: inherit;
    font: inherit;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    -webkit-appearance: none;
    appearance: none;
  }
  .finder input::-webkit-search-cancel-button,
  .finder input::-webkit-search-decoration {
    -webkit-appearance: none;
    appearance: none;
  }
  .finder input::placeholder {
    color: var(--c-sage);
    font-style: italic;
    opacity: 0.9;
  }
  .finder input:focus {
    outline: none;
    border-bottom-color: var(--c-caprock);
    box-shadow: 0 1.5px 0 0 var(--c-caprock);
  }
  /* The "/" that says the field has a key. Only where there is a keyboard
     and a pointer to go with it; gone while the field is in use or holds
     text, so it never sits over what is typed. */
  .finder-key {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    right: 0.75rem;
    top: 50%;
    min-width: 1.45rem;
    padding: 0.1rem 0.3rem;
    border: 1px solid currentColor;
    border-radius: 3px;
    color: inherit;
    font: 0.7rem/1.3 var(--mono);
    text-align: center;
    opacity: 0.5;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    pointer-events: none;
    transition: opacity 0.15s ease;
  }
  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .finder-key {
      display: block;
    }
  }
  .finder-field:focus-within .finder-key,
  .finder input:not(:placeholder-shown) ~ .finder-key {
    opacity: 0;
  }
  .finder-list {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 30;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    top: calc(100% + 0.35rem);
    max-height: 22rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0.25rem 0;
    overflow-y: auto;
    list-style: none;
    background: var(--c-canvas);
    border: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--c-caprock);
    box-shadow: 0 14px 30px -14px rgb(59 56 51 / 0.4);
  }
  .finder-list :global(.opt) {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.8rem;
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .finder-list :global(.opt[aria-selected='true']),
  .finder-list :global(.opt:hover) {
    background: var(--c-mount);
  }
  .finder-list :global(.opt-main) {
    display: block;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }
  .finder-list :global(.opt-see) {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    font-style: italic;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }
  .finder-list :global(.opt-meta),
  .finder-list :global(.opt-kind) {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-sage);
  }
  .finder-list :global(.opt-kind) {
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }
  .finder-list :global(.opt-empty) {
    padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem;
    font-style: italic;
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  /* 3. Holdings --------------------------------------------------- */
  .entries {
    margin-top: 1.6rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
  }
  /* A folder number in the gutter, the record and the shelf indented from
     it: the entry has a left edge to read from, and nothing runs into the
     margins of the page. */
  .entry {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    /* One column, stated. Left implicit, `grid-column: -2` on the entry's
       parts and `1` on its number made TWO columns on a phone - the number
       alone in a second one at the right, the text narrowed beside it. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0 1.5rem;
    padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem 1.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
    transition: background-color 0.25s ease;
  }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .entry {
      grid-template-columns: 4.25rem minmax(0, 1fr);
      padding: 2.75rem 1.75rem 2rem;
    }
  }
  .entry > :not(.entry-mark) {
    grid-column: -2;
  }
  .entry-mark {
    grid-row: 1;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 1rem + 2vw, 2.6rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 0.9;
    font-variant-numeric: lining-nums tabular-nums;
    color: var(--c-haze);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .entry:hover .entry-mark {
    color: rgb(156 91 63 / 0.55);
  }
  @media (max-width: 47.99rem) {
    .entry-mark {
      grid-row: auto;
      grid-column: 1;
      margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
    }
  }
  .entry:hover,
  .entry:focus-within {
    background: rgb(218 211 196 / 0.14);
  }
  .entry:focus-within {
    outline: 2px solid var(--c-caprock);
    outline-offset: 4px;
  }
  /* THE MOUNT TONE. An entry carries a style attribute only when its
     collection has a tone, so [style] is the test: it rests on a band of
     its own hue and its folder number takes the tone. Hover deepens the band
     toward the tone (where color-mix is unknown, the band simply stays). */
  .entry[style] {
    background: var(--m-tint);
  }
  .entry[style]:hover,
  .entry[style]:focus-within {
    background: var(--m-tint);
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--m-tint) 86%, var(--m-mark));
  }
  .entry[style] .entry-mark {
    color: var(--m-mark);
    opacity: 0.6;
    transition: opacity 0.25s ease;
  }
  .entry[style]:hover .entry-mark,
  .entry[style]:focus-within .entry-mark {
    color: var(--m-mark);
    opacity: 1;
  }

  .entry-head {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.75rem 2.5rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .entry-head {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 14rem;
      align-items: end;
    }
  }
  .entry-title {
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 1.1rem + 2vw, 2.5rem);
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.04;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .entry-link {
    color: var(--c-iron);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  /* The whole entry is the link: this lies over all of it. It needs a
     z-index of its own - the prints (which carry a view-transition name) and
     the faded scope note each make a stacking context, and without one they
     sat ON TOP of it, so a click on a photograph went nowhere. */
  .entry-link::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
  }
  .entry-link:focus {
    outline: none;
  }
  .entry:hover .entry-link {
    color: var(--c-caprock);
  }
  .entry-scope {
    max-width: 46ch;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    opacity: 0.88;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  /* The figures, flush right and set in mono, read as a column of record
     values rather than a sentence. */
  .entry-figures {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    line-height: 1.9;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--c-sage);
  }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .entry-figures {
      text-align: right;
    }
  }
  .fig-line {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .fig-line b {
    color: var(--c-iron);
    font-weight: 500;
    font-variant-numeric: lining-nums tabular-nums;
  }
  .fig-quiet b {
    color: var(--c-sage);
  }
  .spark {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    height: 7px;
    margin: 0.35rem 0 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-haze);
  }
  .spark-run,
  .spark-bulk {
    position: absolute;
    left: var(--x);
    width: var(--w);
    min-width: 3px;
  }
  .spark-run {
    top: 2px;
    height: 3px;
    background: rgb(156 91 63 / 0.5);
  }
  .spark-bulk {
    top: 0;
    height: 7px;
    background: var(--c-caprock);
  }

  /* THE SHELF: A STRIP OF THE COLLECTION'S OWN DESK. The record above is
     on the pale band; the prints lie on the dark ground this collection's
     page opens on (--m-ground; the guide's own ground where a collection
     has no tone), run out to the entry's edges. The prints lie in a row
     about one centre line, each at its true shape, and the row is centred:
     the auto margins take up the room when there is room and give way when
     there is not, so a row too long for a phone still starts at the left.
     THE LIGHT is the table's, without a canvas: --wash is the window's
     shaft lying across the strip along the sun's bearing (the lamp's pool
     after dark), --cast / --cast-up the
     shadow each print throws lying and lifted, all set by the script for
     the moment of the visit. What is written here is the strip before it is
     lit, or without script. A margin from a site-wide img rule would push
     the prints off their line, so theirs are pinned. */
  .entry > .shelf {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }
  .shelf {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: clamp(0.75rem, 2.2vw, 1.5rem);
    margin: 1.75rem -1.25rem 0;
    padding: 1.5rem 1.25rem 1.6rem;
    background-color: var(--m-ground, var(--c-ground));
    background-image: var(--wash, none), var(--grain);
    color: var(--m-quiet, var(--c-quiet));
    overflow-x: auto;
    scrollbar-width: none;
  }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .shelf {
      margin: 1.9rem -1.75rem 0;
      padding: 2.1rem 1.75rem 2.2rem;
    }
  }
  .shelf::-webkit-scrollbar {
    display: none;
  }
  .shelf > :first-child {
    margin-left: auto;
  }
  .shelf > :last-child {
    margin-right: auto;
  }
  /* Each print on a narrow off-white mat, as on the table. */
  .plate {
    display: block;
    flex: none;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    margin: 0;
    width: auto;
    padding: clamp(2px, 0.4vw, 5px);
    background: var(--c-mount);
    box-shadow: var(--cast, 0 1px 2px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.5), 0 14px 22px -12px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.7));
    transition:
      transform 0.35s ease,
      box-shadow 0.35s ease;
  }
  /* WIDTH IS CAPPED THROUGH THE HEIGHT, never with max-width. Give a
     replaced element a fixed height and a max-width and the browser does not
     rebalance: it squashes the picture to the narrower box and keeps the
     height, so a landscape print comes out the same shape as a portrait one.
     Each plate carries its own ratio as --ar, and the height is lowered until
     the width fits - so nothing is ever stretched, and a wide print simply
     stands shorter on the shelf, as it would on a table.
     THE BUDGET, at full width: the strip is the entry less its padding
     (52.5rem). The key print at its widest (21rem), three small prints at
     theirs (9rem each) and three gaps (1.5rem each) come to exactly that,
     so nothing is ever pushed off the row. Raise one cap and another must
     come down. */
  .plate--key {
    height: min(clamp(11rem, 30vw, 23rem), calc(min(21rem, 40vw) / var(--ar, 1)));
  }
  .plate--small {
    height: min(clamp(6rem, 16vw, 12rem), calc(min(9rem, 14vw) / var(--ar, 1)));
  }
  /* ON A PHONE the row does not fit, and shrinking it to fit left the
     prints the size of stamps. So it is two rows: the key print, then the
     others beneath it, each far larger than one row allowed. The break
     between them is a zero-height flex item; `order` puts it after the key
     print. Wrapped and centred, the row cannot overrun, so the auto margins
     that centre the single row are not wanted here. */
  @media (max-width: 33.99rem) {
    .shelf {
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      justify-content: center;
      row-gap: 0.5rem;
    }
    .shelf::after {
      content: '';
      flex-basis: 100%;
      order: 1;
    }
    .shelf > :first-child,
    .shelf > :last-child {
      margin-inline: 0;
    }
    .plate--key {
      height: min(clamp(11rem, 62vw, 17rem), calc(68vw / var(--ar, 1)));
    }
    .plate--small {
      order: 2;
      height: min(30vw, calc(22vw / var(--ar, 1)));
    }
  }
  .entry:hover .plate,
  .entry:focus-within .plate {
    transform: translateY(-3px);
    box-shadow: var(--cast-up, 0 2px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.5), 0 24px 34px -16px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.8));
  }
  .plate-empty {
    align-self: center;
    font-style: italic;
    opacity: 0.9;
  }

  /* ON A WIDE SCREEN EACH ENTRY IS A SPREAD, the composition of the desk at
     the head of the page: the record at the left on the pale band, and its
     strip of desk at the right, running the full height of the entry and
     out to its edge. Number | record | strip.
     THE ARITHMETIC THAT KEEPS THE PRINTS ON THE STRIP. --strip is the room
     inside the strip: the page's measure, less the record's column, less
     everything fixed beside it (12.5rem: the entry's left padding, the
     number's column, two gaps, the strip's own padding). The key print may
     take 38% of it, each of three others 16.5%, each of three gaps 3% -
     96.5% in all, at any width; the rest is slack for the prints' mats. A
     print wider than its share stands shorter. */
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .entry {
      --text: clamp(16rem, 27vw, 20rem);
      --strip: calc(var(--fw) - var(--text) - 12.5rem);
      grid-template-columns: 4.25rem var(--text) minmax(0, 1fr);
      grid-template-rows: 1fr auto;
      padding: 0 0 0 1.75rem;
    }
    .entry-mark {
      padding-top: 2.6rem;
    }
    .entry > .entry-head {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 1;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      align-content: start;
      align-items: start;
      gap: 1.4rem;
      padding-top: 2.5rem;
    }
    .entry > .entry-foot {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 2;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.5rem;
      margin-top: 1.4rem;
      padding-bottom: 2.1rem;
    }
    .entry > .shelf {
      grid-column: 3;
      grid-row: 1 / -1;
      align-self: stretch;
      gap: calc(var(--strip) * 0.03);
      margin: 0;
      padding: 2.25rem 1.75rem;
    }
    .entry-title {
      font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 1.1rem + 1.3vw, 2.35rem);
    }
    .entry-figures {
      text-align: left;
    }
    .plate--key {
      height: min(23rem, calc(var(--strip) * 0.38 / var(--ar, 1)));
    }
    .plate--small {
      height: min(11.5rem, calc(var(--strip) * 0.165 / var(--ar, 1)));
    }
  }

  .entry-foot {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.3rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
